From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u, user32, winex11, wintab32: a pen that comes after a program started, and goes again

A pen's device can appear after programs started: a Bluetooth pen switched
on, a Surface's iptsd starting after the session, a tablet plugged in.
wine-sg 1150 already reads X's pens again when its devices change
(XI_HierarchyChanged), so a running program's windows get the new pen's
WM_POINTER messages with its pressure -- tested again here, plainly and in
the shell's desktop. What was still missing:

- RegisterPointerDeviceNotifications was a stub: a program was never told
  of a pen coming or going (drawing programs list the devices again on
  WM_POINTERDEVICECHANGE). Now each change of the device list is
  WM_POINTERDEVICECHANGE (PDC_ARRIVAL / PDC_REMOVAL) to the registered
  windows. The range messages (WM_POINTERDEVICEINRANGE/OUTOFRANGE) are not
  sent.
- Wintab read the tablet once, when a program first asked: with no pen then,
  Wintab had no tablet for the rest of the program's life. Once a pen is
  listed it is read again (at most twice a second), so a program asking
  again finds the tablet, opens a context and gets its packets.
- A pen whose device went (unplugged mid-stroke, switched off) stayed in
  range over its window for ever: X says no more of it. Its pointer now
  leaves the window (WM_POINTERLEAVE), once per process.

The SG PDF pen check (sg-shell test/pdf-pen-check.sh) had worked around a
late pen by starting SG PDF again after the pen appeared; it now plugs the
test tablet in after SG PDF started.

Test: test/latepen-gate.sh (make test-latepen; sg-compositor 0.2.0+sg46's
test tablet with "plug" and "unplug", test/latepen-probe.c started before
the tablet comes, plainly and in the shell's desktop; 22 checks). Mutants,
each failing it: SG_MUTANT_LATEPEN_NO_NOTIFY (win32u input.c),
LATEPEN_WINTAB_ONCE (wintab32 context.c), LATEPEN_NO_LEAVE (winex11
mouse.c), NO_PEN_HOTPLUG (1150's, winex11 mouse.c).
